A||||10||WisconsinGT||It Died!||||1||
Z||000000||WisconsinGT||05-12-2001||10:03 PM||||Well folks, my 88GT bit the dust. I'm driving in the country just getting her up to 60 mph when she just shut down. It turns over, but it will not start. Anyone know what can be wrong? I have a feeling that I lost my timing chain. <BR>Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000001||sluppy123||05-12-2001||10:27 PM||||It sounds like the ignition module, when mine went the car stopped dead in it's tracks, turned over but no spark. <P>You can check the timing chain by cranking the engine while looking into the oil fill hole on the valve cover, if there's movement your alright. <P>------------------<BR>-Brian, Toolmaker<BR>Lincoln, NE<BR>85' SC & 87' GT-Galaxy silver mettalic, Eibach springs, RSM side scoops & chin spoiler, Mr Mikes leather, 70hp NOS, & much more.<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=247832&a=12781563&p=48213929&Sequence=0"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||maryjane||05-12-2001||11:11 PM||||I agree with Sluppy. My 84 did the same thing without warning two weeks after we bought it. Some ignition modules can be tested, but once you take it out I think I would go ahead and replace it, have the old one tested and if it's good, keep it for a spare. With the eng compartment temps a Fiero generates, it is not unusual for one to fail. Mine cost a lonng walk and a tow bill because I didn't have a spare.<BR>Don||||reg||14
Z||000003||Oreif||05-13-2001||01:16 AM||||Pull the ignition module and any NAPA can check it. If it's good, it's the pick-up coil inside the distributor. Mine died while I was cruising at 40mph. It just shut down. Pick-up coil was bad. The module and pick-up are famous (or should I say infamous) for dying abruptly due to heat. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||V8Fiero||05-13-2001||01:22 AM||||Dont overlook the fuel pump. Can you hear it run for a few seconds when you turn on the key?<P>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000005||Old Lar||05-13-2001||08:27 AM||||I had a fuel pump fuse blow in the middle of nowhere at night far far from home.  Had to get the car towed to a Pep Boys. Spend the night on a motel. They did some diagnostics and found no juice to the fuel pump. (I had a new distributor in) The mechanic felt guilty charging $50 + tax for a 25 cent fuse, but ..  So with the towing, motel it was expensive.||||reg||3
Z||000006||WisconsinGT||05-13-2001||09:36 PM||||Thanks for the reply's. I have a new 8 month old distrbuter that came with a module. I got it from AutoZone. The funny thing about the module that came with it is that it has a thin piece of metal covering it. I would think that it would create more heat for the module. Anyway, I do hear the pump click shortly after I turn the key to the on position. Sluppy, I will look in the oil fill hole. Thnaks!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||V8Fiero||05-13-2001||09:39 PM||||The click you are hearing is the fuel pump relay. You should hear a humm coming from underneath the center console. Should run for about 5 secs maybe.<BR>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000008||Raydar||05-13-2001||10:07 PM||||It also could be a bad coil. I've heard that they cause a sputter before they die, but I wouldn't depend on that being the case every time.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 Duke coupe <BR>88 "wadded up" Formula (parts car)||||reg||1
Z||000009||WisconsinGT||05-14-2001||11:30 PM||||Update!!! I bought a new control modual. (74.00 thanks GM) Put it in and the car started up. Then I drove it back to my house and it died again. The car will not start again. Anyone have any ideas what the problem could be? Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000010||Fierowrecker||05-15-2001||07:30 AM||||Pay attention...<BR>It takes three things to run an engine...<BR>Air, fuel and spark...<BR>Have a friend crank over the engine, if you get spark, then check fuel pressure...<BR>Course, you could check the codes, I had a TPS fail, had spark and fuel, but would not start...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
